<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ion Antonescu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1882">1882</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ion Antonescu was a Romanian army officer who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> as Conducător from 1940 to 1944. Appointed prime minister by Carol II in 1940, he forced the king's abdication, crushed the Iron Guard in 1941 and took Romania into the war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> as an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, while his regime deported and killed hundreds of thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a>. He was arrested in King Michael's coup of August 1944 and executed in 1946.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ion-antonescu.jpg" alt="Ion Antonescu" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ion_Antonescu_1941.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ion Antonescu was born on 14 June 1882 in Pitești, into an upper-middle-class Romanian Orthodox family with some military tradition. His father, an army officer, sent him to the Infantry and Cavalry School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/craiova">Craiova</a>. He made his name as an army officer in the peasants' revolt of 1907 and in the Romanian campaign of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, when he served as chief of staff to General Constantin Prezan. Between the wars he was military attaché to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and later Chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">General Staff</a>, and he served as Defence Minister in the cabinet of Octavian Goga and the first Cristea cabinet. His sympathy with far-right politics brought him into conflict with King Carol II and led to his detention.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the crisis of 1940 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-dobruja">Southern Dobruja</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and Northern Transylvania to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> pressure, and Carol's popularity collapsed. Antonescu left his assigned residence and negotiated with Iuliu Maniu. The National Legionary State, proclaimed on 14 September 1940, made the Iron Guard the only legal party, with Antonescu as Prime Minister and Conducător and Horia Sima as his deputy. After Hitler told him in January 1941 of the plan to invade the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and judged him the more competent leader, Antonescu crushed the Guard in the Legionary Rebellion of January 1941 and ruled as a military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>. Romania joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-barbarossa">Operation Barbarossa</a> and recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a> and Northern Bukovina, and he became Marshal of Romania.</p><p>His regime's policies were responsible for the deaths of as many as 400,000 people, mostly Bessarabian, Ukrainian and Romanian Jews, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani</a>. They combined pogroms and mass murders, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odessa">Odessa</a> massacre, with ethnic cleansing and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> to occupied Transnistria. Odessa fell in October 1941 after a two-month siege. The system was inconsistent: it put plunder before killing, treated most Jews of the Old Kingdom with leniency, and in the end refused the Final Solution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Allied air attacks on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and heavy losses on the Eastern Front led Antonescu to open inconclusive peace talks in 1944. On 23 August 1944 King Michael I led a coup against him and had him arrested. Tried by a People's Tribunal in May 1946 for war crimes, crimes against the peace and treason, he was executed on 1 June 1946. The tribunals were comparable to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg-trials">Nuremberg trials</a>, though the procedures were politicised in favour of the Romanian Communist Party.</p><p>Under communist rule the documentation of the massacres came chiefly from Jewish Romanians, such as Matatias Carp's Black Book of 1946–1948. In 2003 an international commission led by Elie Wiesel studied the Holocaust in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, and its final report brought official recognition of Antonescu's part in it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ion Antonescu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Carol II, increasingly unpopular, was forced to abdicate and named General <b>Ion Antonescu</b> prime minister with full powers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-romania">Kingdom of Romania · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In Romania the Iron Guard came to power with General <b>Ion Antonescu</b> in 1940, the only fascist movement outside Germany and Italy to do so without foreign help, and was repressed the next year.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/fascism">Fascism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ion Antonescu</b> led it into war as an Axis ally until King Michael's coup of August 1944.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-romania">Kingdom of Romania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pitești, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. Died in Jilava, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.87,24.88&amp;year=1923" aria-label="Pitești on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5143 579 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ion Antonescu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5593 849h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5593 849h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.87,24.88&amp;year=1923">What was here in 1923? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
